<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ravidas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. 15th–16th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Raidas, Ravidass</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ravidas was an Indian mystic poet-saint of the Bhakti movement who lived in the 15th and 16th centuries, though the details of his life are uncertain. Born near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Varanasi</a> to parents of the leather-working Chamar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a>, treated as untouchable, he taught the removal of divisions of caste and gender and worshipped God as nirguna, without attributes. Forty-one of his poems are in the Adi Granth, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> scripture, and tradition names the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> princess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mirabai">Mirabai</a> as his disciple. He is the central figure of the Ravidassia religious movement.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ravidas.jpg" alt="Ravidas" width="320" height="358" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ravidas,_detail_of_a_painting_of_Kabir_with_Ravidas,_Mughal,_1625_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
